      "dc:title": "Effects of opencut coal mining on water quality and movement",
      "dc:creator": "Ano ",
      "prism:publicationName": "undefined",
      "prism:pageRange": null,
      "prism:coverDate": "1989-01-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "1989",
      "dc:description": "In broad regional terms, opencut coal mining in the Hunter Valley is currently not having any major detrimental impacts on water quality and movement, nor is it interfering significantly with other water resource uses and users. There is a shortage of irrigation water in streams draining the Ashford and Gunnedah coalfields: open cut mining in no way contributes to this shortage. Potential salinity and acid mine discharge problems are recognised at mines in the Oaklands and Gloucester Basins. There is considerable scope for a rationalisation of water quality monitoring activities between the various authorities. Baseline data being measured. However until data are interpreted, the adequacy of the monitoring program remains unknown. Importantly, such data are not being meaningfully incorporated into the management process. The major potential future water quality problem is seen to be the long term stability and viability of rehabilitated spoil areas. -from Author",
