      "dc:title": "Planning, operational and environmental aspects of open cut mining at Boundary Hill Mine, Central Queensland",
      "dc:creator": "Parkin R.",
      "prism:publicationName": "Mining Engineer London",
      "prism:issn": "00265179",
      "prism:volume": "148",
      "prism:issueIdentifier": "327",
      "prism:pageRange": "268-275",
      "prism:coverDate": "1988-12-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "December 1988",
      "dc:description": "The author discusses the mine planning functions of an open cut truck-and-shovel operation at Boundary Hill Mine in Central Queensland. These planning functions include geology, surveying, mining engineering, and coal quality. Boundary Hill Mine was the first truck-and-shovel operation in Central Queensland. It was the first to have an inpit coal crusher, where the coal is conveyed out of the pit via a secondary crusher into a live reinforced earth bunker. The following environmental aspects have been outlined: (i) pre-mining environment - which includes climate, rainfall, temperature, topography, natural vegetation, fauna, land use and capability; (ii) post-mining environment - rehabilitation, water management, dust, noise and ground vibration.",
