      "dc:title": "AUSTRALIA'S COAL RUSH CONTINUES.",
      "dc:creator": "Anon",
      "prism:publicationName": "Australian Mining",
      "prism:issn": "0004976X",
      "prism:volume": "67",
      "prism:issueIdentifier": "4",
      "prism:pageRange": "13-16",
      "prism:coverDate": "1975-01-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "Apr",
      "dc:description": "In recent years coal mining has been characterized by mechanization of underground mining, a rapid advance in open cut mining and a vast development in exports, particularly of coking coal to Japan. As well as supplying 27 million tons of black coal to the home market, Australian coal mining provides a 4. 7% slice of the country's exports and 15% of all Australia's exports to Japan. At a time of a world fuel shortage, Australia, with enormous coal reserves, can develop this already great industry even more. Reserves of black and brown coal are such that current or even increased production could last for hundreds of years even if only known, first-grade reserves are mined.",
